"Improving support with every interaction at OpenAI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/openai-support-model/)\nciteturn3425view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-support-model/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 192\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†API OpenAI on OpenAI\nL16: # Improving support with every interaction at OpenAI\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: More than tickets, a new operating model\nL23: \nL24:   * cite4†More than tickets, a new operating model L25:   * cite5†Connecting a system of interactions L26:   * cite6†Support reps as systems thinkers  L27:   * cite7†From primitives to production L28:   * cite8†Learning that compounds L29:   * cite9†A blueprint for the future of support L30:   * cite4†More than tickets, a new operating model L31:   * cite5†Connecting a system of interactions L32:   * cite6†Support reps as systems thinkers  L33:   * cite7†From primitives to production L34:   * cite8†Learning that compounds L35:   * cite9†A blueprint for the future of support L36: This is part of our series sharing internal examples of how OpenAI is using its own technology and APIs. These tools are being used internally, only at OpenAI, and are shared here as illustrative examples of how frontier AI is supporting use cases across our teams. We’re also sharing the internal tool names for a clearer look at how frontier AI helps our teams get work done.\nL37: ## More than tickets, a new operating model\nL38: \nL39: Support has historically meant queues, tickets, and throughput. But at OpenAI, that wasn’t enough. We serve hundreds of millions of users, handle millions of requests each year, and see that volume grow by multiples annually.\nL40: Lots of organizations deal with scale. Fewer deal with scale and hypergrowth. Almost none face both—while also building the very technology that could change the equation. That combination uniquely positioned us to rethink support from the ground up.\nL41: \nL42: > “Support has never really been about replying to just tickets. It’s about whether people get what they need, whether it actually serves them well.”\nL43: \nL44: Glen Worthington, Head of User Ops\nL45: Support isn’t a volume challenge. It’s an engineering and operational design challenge. So we built something different: an operating model where every interaction improves the next.\nL46: ## Connecting a system of interactions\nL47: \nL48: The Ops team wanted to go well beyond using a chatbot to deflect support questions. The team has a vision: reimagine support as an AI operating model that continuously learns and improves.\nL49: \nL50: At the center are three building blocks:\nL51: \nL52:   * Surfaces. Where support systems are interacted with. Chat, email, and phone, but increasingly, help embedded directly inside the product.\nL53:   * Knowledge. Not just static docs, but living and continuously improving guidance drawn from real conversations, policies, and context.\nL54: \nL55:   * Evals and classifiers. Shared definitions of quality built by software and humans in unison, plus tools to measure, improve, and highlight feedback.\nL56: These pieces don’t sit in isolation. They form a loop. A pattern spotted in an enterprise conversation can inform a developer FAQ. An eval written for one case strengthens the model for thousands more. And because the same primitives power every surface - chat, email, voice—improvements scale across channels automatically.\nL57: ## Support reps as systems thinkers\nL58: \nL59: The role of a support rep is changing. Our aim is to shift the model from primarily focusing on processing transactional work to being a part of the overall build. They’re empowered to contribute to the architecture itself, both directly through bottom up shipping of changes and indirectly through the natural motions of their day to day work.\nL60: Reps flag interactions that should become test cases, propose and ship classifiers when they see new patterns, and even prototype lightweight automations to close workflow gaps in days. Training shifts too, it’s not just about policies, but about evaluating interactions, identifying structural gaps, and feeding improvements back.\nL61: \nL62: The new approach strives to ensure that support reps are builders as much as responders.\nL63: > “Agents aren’t just responding to tickets. They’re informing our knowledge base and our policies. They have an ear to the ground that we don’t.”\nL64: \nL65: Shimul Sachdeva, Engineering Manager\nL66: \nL67: The result is a support organization defined less by throughput and more by its capacity to evolve. Every person is not only serving users but also actively improving the machinery that serves all users.\nL68: ## From primitives to production\nL69: \nL70: Building support this way is only possible because we’re built on OpenAI’s stack.\nL71: \nL72:   * Agents SDK gives us step-level traces and observability by default. We can replay runs, inspect tool calls, and debug root causes instantly.\nL73: \nL74:   * Responses API powers classifiers for tone, correctness, and policy adherence.\nL75: \nL76:   * Realtime API makes voice support possible.\nL77: \nL78:   * OpenAI’s Evals dashboard makes quality measurable and easy to visualize over time.\nL79: Because the platform primitives come ready-made, we spend less time stitching systems together and more time focusing on the work that matters: defining what good looks like, measuring it, and improving it.\nL80: We started with a simple Q&A answerer that worked well. With Agents SDK, we quickly expanded into dynamic actions for things like refunds, invoices, incident lookups. As the models continue to improve with larger context windows, Deep Research, and stronger agentic capabilities, we can adopt those advances immediately.\nL81: ## Learning that compounds\nL82: \nL83: Evals turn everyday conversations into production tests. They codify what “great” means—not just solving the issue, but doing it politely, clearly, and consistently. Reps play a direct role here, flagging strong and weak examples that become evals, and those evals run continuously in production to steer model behavior.\nL84: “Usually when you have a problem, you just want help as quickly as possible. By using our AI tools, we’re able to get those responses much more quickly—and just as importantly, we know when the model shouldn’t answer,” says Jay Patel, Software Engineer, Support Automation.\nL85: Learning doesn’t stop at resolution. Patterns feed back into knowledge, automation, and product design. The system compounds: faster answers for users, tighter feedback loops for builders, and a consistently higher bar for quality across every surface.\nL86: And it’s not just the AI that learns. The organization learns alongside it. Specialists see where models fall short, shape new classifiers, and contribute datasets for fine-tuning. Observability dashboards make quality measurable, showing how performance improves over time.\nL87: ## A blueprint for the future of support\nL88: \nL89: The most profound shift isn’t the tooling, it’s the people and how the organization measures success. Support specialists are recognized not just for solving problems, but for refining knowledge, improving models, and extending the system itself. Leaders look for a new kind of teammate: someone who pairs frontline empathy with design instincts, combining support craft with curiosity to improve the system.\nL90: > “We’re starting to see this marriage between deep craft expertise and deep engineering expertise. That’s the future of how departments run.”\nL91: \nL92: Glen Worthington, Head of User Ops\nL93: \nL94: And our vision is support stops being a destination you go to. It becomes an action, woven into every product surface. Users don’t “open a ticket.” They simply get what they need, where they are.\nL95: What began as a response to scale has become a blueprint for how people and AI can work together: collaborative, adaptive, and continuously improving.\nL96: ## Ready to put ChatGPT to work in your business?\nL97: \nL98: cite10†Talk with our team L99: ## Keep reading\nL100: \nL101: cite11†View all L102: \nL103: cite12†Empowering teams to unlock insights faster at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L104: \nL105: cite13†Turning contracts into searchable data at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L106: \nL107: cite14†Converting inbound leads into customers at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L108: \nL109: Research\nL110: \nL111:   * cite15†Research Index L112:   * cite16†Research Overview L113:   * cite17†Economic Research L114: \nL115: Latest Advancements\nL116: \nL117:   * cite18†GPT-6.1 Sol L118:   * cite19†GPT-6 Astra L119:   * cite20†GPT-5.6 L120:   * cite21†GPT-5.5 L121: \nL122: Safety\nL123:   * cite22†Safety Approach L124:   * cite23†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L125:   * cite24†Security & Privacy L126:   * cite25†Trust & Transparency L127: \nL128: Products\nL129:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L130:   * cite26†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L131:   * cite27†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L132:   * cite28†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L133:   * cite29†Codex†chatgpt.com L134:   * cite30†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L135:   * cite31†Release Notes L136: \nL137: API Platform\nL138: \nL139:   * cite32†Overview L140:   * cite33†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L141:   * cite34†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L142: \nL143: Business\nL144:   * cite35†Overview L145:   * cite36†Solutions L146:   * cite37†Resources L147:   * cite38†Plugins L148:   * cite39†Customer Stories L149:   * cite40†Partner Network L150:   * cite10†Contact Sales L151: \nL152: Developers\nL153: \nL154:   * cite41†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L155:   * cite42†Open Models L156:   * cite43†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L157:   * cite44†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L158:   * cite45†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L159: \nL160: Company\nL161:   * cite46†About Us L162:   * cite47†Our Charter L163:   * cite48†Careers L164:   * cite11†News L165: \nL166: Support\nL167: \nL168:   * cite49†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L169:   * cite50†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L170: \nL171: More\nL172: \nL173:   * cite51†Stories L174:   * cite52†Academy L175:   * cite53†Supply Co. L176:   * cite54†Livestreams L177:   * cite55†Podcast L178:   * cite56†RSS L179: \nL180: Terms & Policies\nL181: \nL182:   * cite57†Terms of Use L183:   * cite58†Privacy Policy L184:   * cite59†Other Policies L185: cite60†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L186: \nL187: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL188: \nL189: English United States\nL190: \nL191: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConverting inbound leads into customers at OpenAI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/openai-inbound-sales-assistant/)\nciteturn3425view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-inbound-sales-assistant/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 187\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†API OpenAI on OpenAI\nL16: # Converting inbound leads into customers at OpenAI\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: Building the inbound sales assistant\nL23: \nL24:   * cite4†Building the inbound sales assistant L25:   * cite5†Built with reps, for reps L26:   * cite6†From missed leads to high growth  L27:   * cite7†A new standard for engagement L28: \nL29:   * cite4†Building the inbound sales assistant L30:   * cite5†Built with reps, for reps L31:   * cite6†From missed leads to high growth  L32:   * cite7†A new standard for engagement L33: This is part of our series sharing internal examples of how OpenAI is using its own technology and APIs. These tools are being used internally, only at OpenAI, and are shared here as illustrative examples of how frontier AI is supporting use cases across our teams. We’re also sharing the internal tool names for a clearer look at how frontier AI helps our teams get work done.\nL34: When ChatGPT Enterprise and Business each launched, inbound demand surged. Tens of thousands of companies—from early-stage startups to multinational enterprises—were reaching out every month. The demand was remarkable. The strain on our systems was real.\nL35: Routing those leads through forms and static workflows couldn’t meet the moment. Too many prospects got an automated reply telling them to sign up online. Too few had their questions answered. The result was missed opportunities and a buying experience that didn’t match the trust customers were placing in us.\nL36: \nL37: The challenge wasn’t just scale. It was quality. Buyers wanted specific answers:\nL38: \nL39:   * Is this product compliant in a healthcare environment?\nL40: \nL41:   * How do we compare plans and choose the right one?\nL42:   * What results are peers in our industry seeing?\nL43: \nL44: “We were getting thousands of leads a month and only had capacity to talk to a small fraction. Some leads needed a couple of questions answered to really make a great buying experience, but we weren’t able to provide that personalized experience,” says Harsha Chilakamarri, Go-to-Market Innovation.\nL45: \nL46: Traditional automation couldn’t carry that nuance. Hiring linearly wasn’t sustainable. We needed a different approach.\nL47: ## Building the inbound sales assistant\nL48: \nL49: We created an AI-powered inbound sales assistant designed not to replace reps, but to extend their reach—trained and refined with rep feedback.\nL50: \nL51: At its core are our internal connectors. Product documentation, policy libraries, customer stories, and playbooks are pulled into context the model can reason over. The assistant doesn’t guess. It responds with accuracy, in the prospect’s language, directly tied to their question.\nL52: That means prospects get a personalized response within minutes, written in their own language, grounded in their actual question.\nL53: \nL54:   * A company in Tokyo receives an answer in Japanese, not an English form letter.\nL55: \nL56:   * A hospital system asking about compliance gets the details in their first exchange, not after days of waiting.\nL57: \nL58:   * If the prospect is enterprise-qualified, the thread is seamlessly handed off to a rep, with context intact.\nL59: “This model allows us to engage with and provide every customer a hyper personalized experience,” says Chilakamarri.\nL60: \nL61: This isn’t automation for its own sake. It’s automation that delivers value, right away.\nL62: ## Built with reps, for reps\nL63: \nL64: The breakthrough wasn’t just the assistant’s first reply. It was the loop behind it.\nL65: \nL66: When training the model, every draft response went back to sales reps for corrections. Every correction became training data. Accuracy climbed from 60 percent to more than 98 percent within weeks. Instead of generic templates, the assistant started to sound like the best version of our team, codifying judgment and making it available at scale.\nL67: > “We built a very complex eval system with just me and one other engineer… Once we had a way to do those evals, especially in an automated fashion, we were able to quickly go from 60% accuracy to 90%, and now 98% on first emails.”\nL68: \nL69: Harsha Chilakamarri, Go-to-Market Innovation\nL70: \nL71: For reps, the shift was immediate. Inboxes weren’t clogged with unqualified leads. They opened conversations already in motion, with prospects who had real intent and real questions answered.\nL72: The evals also gave leadership confidence. They showed measurable progress, not just anecdotes. They proved the assistant could be scaled responsibly.\nL73: ## From missed leads to high growth\nL74: \nL75: The impact was immediate. A small company once lost in the queue submitted questions, got thoughtful answers within hours, and signed an enterprise contract days later. Those stories repeated again and again.\nL76: \nL77: What had been a dead end became one of our strongest growth channels. Within months, multimillions in annual recurring revenue were unlocked.\nL78: > “Our biggest aha moment was when we first launched the assistant. We realized that if we give inbound leads personalized experiences and quickly answer key questions—even over email—many are eager to buy really quickly.”\nL79: \nL80: Harsha Chilakamarri, Go-to-Market Innovation\nL81: \nL82: For reps getting passed qualified leads, the shift was just as valuable. Instead of digging through generic leads, they saw active conversations with clear intent. For the first time, no one felt left behind.\nL83: ## A new standard for engagement\nL84: \nL85: This isn’t only about inbound leads. It points to a broader opportunity: onboarding, renewals, and support can all benefit from trusted, personalized conversations.\nL86: \nL87: The lesson is simple: when you scale the excellence of your best reps through AI, you change what’s possible for the entire team.\nL88: \nL89: As Chilakamarri put it: “Leadership could not be more excited by this. It’s proof that we can build OpenAI on OpenAI and showcase our technology directly to customers.”\nL90: Personalizing every lead isn’t a tactic. It’s becoming a better way for all engagement.\nL91: ## Ready to put ChatGPT to work in your business?\nL92: \nL93: cite8†Talk with our team L94: ## Keep reading\nL95: \nL96: cite9†View all L97: \nL98: cite10†Driving sales productivity and customer success at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L99: \nL100: cite11†Turning contracts into searchable data at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L101: \nL102: cite12†Improving support with every interaction at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L103: \nL104: Research\nL105: \nL106:   * cite13†Research Index L107:   * cite14†Research Overview L108:   * cite15†Economic Research L109: \nL110: Latest Advancements\nL111: \nL112:   * cite16†GPT-6.1 Sol L113:   * cite17†GPT-6 Astra L114:   * cite18†GPT-5.6 L115:   * cite19†GPT-5.5 L116: \nL117: Safety\nL118:   * cite20†Safety Approach L119:   * cite21†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L120:   * cite22†Security & Privacy L121:   * cite23†Trust & Transparency L122: \nL123: Products\nL124:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L125:   * cite24†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L126:   * cite25†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L127:   * cite26†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L128:   * cite27†Codex†chatgpt.com L129:   * cite28†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L130:   * cite29†Release Notes L131: \nL132: API Platform\nL133: \nL134:   * cite30†Overview L135:   * cite31†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L136:   * cite32†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L137: \nL138: Business\nL139:   * cite33†Overview L140:   * cite34†Solutions L141:   * cite35†Resources L142:   * cite36†Plugins L143:   * cite37†Customer Stories L144:   * cite38†Partner Network L145:   * cite8†Contact Sales L146: \nL147: Developers\nL148: \nL149:   * cite39†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L150:   * cite40†Open Models L151:   * cite41†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L152:   * cite42†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L153:   * cite43†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L154: \nL155: Company\nL156:   * cite44†About Us L157:   * cite45†Our Charter L158:   * cite46†Careers L159:   * cite9†News L160: \nL161: Support\nL162: \nL163:   * cite47†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L164:   * cite48†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L165: \nL166: More\nL167: \nL168:   * cite49†Stories L169:   * cite50†Academy L170:   * cite51†Supply Co. L171:   * cite52†Livestreams L172:   * cite53†Podcast L173:   * cite54†RSS L174: \nL175: Terms & Policies\nL176: \nL177:   * cite55†Terms of Use L178:   * cite56†Privacy Policy L179:   * cite57†Other Policies L180: cite58†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite59†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L181: \nL182: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL183: \nL184: English United States\nL185: \nL186: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTurning contracts into searchable data at OpenAI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/openai-contract-data-agent/)\nciteturn3425view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-contract-data-agent/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 180\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†API OpenAI on OpenAI\nL16: # Turning contracts into searchable data at OpenAI\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: When contracts became the bottleneck\nL23: \nL24:   * cite4†When contracts became the bottleneck L25:   * cite5†Building a smarter workflow L26:   * cite6†Confident contract reviews L27:   * cite7†Beyond contracts L28:   * cite8†A new operating model for finance L29: \nL30:   * cite4†When contracts became the bottleneck L31:   * cite5†Building a smarter workflow L32:   * cite6†Confident contract reviews L33:   * cite7†Beyond contracts L34:   * cite8†A new operating model for finance L35: This is part of our series sharing internal examples of how OpenAI is using its own technology and APIs. These tools are being used internally, only at OpenAI, and are shared here as illustrative examples of how frontier AI is supporting use cases across our teams. We’re also sharing the internal tool names for a clearer look at how frontier AI helps our teams get work done.\nL36: ## When contracts became the bottleneck\nL37: \nL38: Every enterprise deal comes with a signed contract. Each one has start dates, billing terms, renewal clauses.\nL39: At first, the process was manageable: read line by line, retype into a spreadsheet, move on. But when volume doubled and doubled again, this manual approach broke.\nL40: “In less than six months, the team went from reviewing hundreds of contracts each month to more than a thousand. And yet we’d only hired one new person. It was obvious that the process wasn’t going to scale,” says Wei An Lee, AI Engineer.\nL41: ## Building a smarter workflow\nL42: \nL43: Instead of throwing more people at the problem, our finance and engineering teams built a contract data agent. The design principle was simple: take the repetition out of contract review, keep experts firmly in control.\nL44: \nL45: The Agent works in three steps:\nL46: \nL47:   * Ingest data: PDFs, scanned copies, even phone photos marked up with handwritten edits. What used to be dozens of inconsistent files now flow into one pipeline.\nL48:   * Inference with prompting: Using retrieval-augmented prompting, the system parses contracts into structured data. It doesn’t dump a thousand pages into context; it pulls only what’s relevant, reasons against it, and shows its work.\nL49: \nL50:   * Review: Finance experts review the structured output, complete with annotations and references for any non-standard terms. The agent highlights what’s unusual; humans are then looped in to review.\nL51: > “We’re not just parsing, we’re reasoning—showing why a term is considered non-standard, citing the reference material, and letting the reviewer confirm the ASC 606 classification.”\nL52: \nL53: Siddharth Jain, AI Engineer\nL54: ## Confident contract reviews\nL55: \nL56: The output is a dataset that’s immediately useful across finance workflows. What once took hours arrives overnight, annotated and ready for validation. Experts remain in the loop, but their role shifts from manual entry to judgment.\nL57: \nL58: > “The amazing thing is that the heavy lifting happens with AI—and then our teams wake up in the morning to data that’s ready for them to review.”\nL59: \nL60: Wei An Lee, AI Engineer\nL61: This design ensures confidence: professionals get structured, reasoned data at scale, but their expertise drives the outcome.\nL62: \nL63: The results:\nL64: \nL65:   * Faster turnaround. Reviews cut in half, ready overnight.\nL66: \nL67:   * Higher capacity. Thousands of contracts processed without expanding headcount in lockstep.\nL68: \nL69:   * Smarter context. Non-standard terms flagged with reasoning and references.\nL70: \nL71:   * Queryable results. Tabular output in the data warehouse allows for easier data analysis.\nL72: Each cycle of human feedback sharpens the Agent, making every review faster and more accurate.\nL73: \nL74: “The only way we can scale as OpenAI scales is through this,” Wei An said. “Without it, you’d have to grow your team linearly in lockstep with contract volume. This lets us keep the team lean while handling hypergrowth.”\nL75: ## Beyond contracts\nL76: \nL77: This architecture now supports procurement, compliance, even month-end close. The same principle applies: automate the rote work, keep humans in charge of judgment.\nL78: \nL79: Engineers describe it as “manual work already done,” not decisions replaced. Finance teams still write the story of the numbers; the Agent ensures they don’t spend their day doing painstaking work.\nL80: ## A new operating model for finance\nL81: \nL82: What started as a fix for contracts has become a new way of working in finance. Data parsing runs overnight. Professionals focus on analysis and strategy. Leaders scale confidently with growth, without growing teams in lockstep.\nL83: The contract data Agent is a blueprint for how AI can responsibly transform regulated, high-stakes work. It shows what becomes possible when experts partner with intelligent systems: more leverage, more confidence, and more time spent on what matters most.\nL84: ## Ready to put ChatGPT to work in your business?\nL85: \nL86: cite9†Talk with our team L87: ## Keep reading\nL88: \nL89: cite10†View all L90: \nL91: cite11†Driving sales productivity and customer success at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L92: \nL93: cite12†Improving support with every interaction at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L94: \nL95: cite13†Converting inbound leads into customers at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L96: \nL97: Research\nL98: \nL99:   * cite14†Research Index L100:   * cite15†Research Overview L101:   * cite16†Economic Research L102: \nL103: Latest Advancements\nL104: \nL105:   * cite17†GPT-6.1 Sol L106:   * cite18†GPT-6 Astra L107:   * cite19†GPT-5.6 L108:   * cite20†GPT-5.5 L109: \nL110: Safety\nL111:   * cite21†Safety Approach L112:   * cite22†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L113:   * cite23†Security & Privacy L114:   * cite24†Trust & Transparency L115: \nL116: Products\nL117:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L118:   * cite25†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L119:   * cite26†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L120:   * cite27†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L121:   * cite28†Codex†chatgpt.com L122:   * cite29†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L123:   * cite30†Release Notes L124: \nL125: API Platform\nL126: \nL127:   * cite31†Overview L128:   * cite32†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L129:   * cite33†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L130: \nL131: Business\nL132:   * cite34†Overview L133:   * cite35†Solutions L134:   * cite36†Resources L135:   * cite37†Plugins L136:   * cite38†Customer Stories L137:   * cite39†Partner Network L138:   * cite9†Contact Sales L139: \nL140: Developers\nL141: \nL142:   * 